---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import GamePlayer from '../../components/GamePlayer.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
import games from '../../data/games.json';
import skills from '../../data/skills.json';
import { url } from '../../config';

const game = games.find(g => g.id === 'count-objects-prek')!;
const skill = skills[game.skill as keyof typeof skills];
const page = new URL(url('math-games/counting-for-preschool/'), Astro.site).href;
const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'LearningResource', name: `${game.keywordTitle}: ${game.title}`,
    url: page, description: game.summary, isAccessibleForFree: true, inLanguage: 'en',
    learningResourceType: 'Educational game', educationalLevel: 'Preschool', typicalAgeRange: '3-5',
    teaches: 'Counting objects up to 10', timeRequired: 'PT3M', image: new URL(url(game.poster), Astro.site).href,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList', itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: new URL(url(), Astro.site).href },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Math games', item: new URL(url('math-games/'), Astro.site).href },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: game.keywordTitle, item: page },
    ],
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="Counting Games for Preschool: Free Count to 10 Game for Ages 3 to 5 | EduLearnLabs"
  description="A free counting game for ages 3 to 5. Kids tap apples, pebbles and acorns one by one, say how many and grow a parade of baby animals. Gentle hints, no ads, no login."
  path="math-games/counting-for-preschool/" nav="math" image={game.poster} jsonLd={jsonLd}>

  <Breadcrumbs back={{ name: 'Math games', path: 'math-games/' }}
    items={[{ name: 'Home', path: '' }, { name: 'Math games', path: 'math-games/' }, { name: game.keywordTitle, path: 'math-games/counting-for-preschool/' }]} />

  <h1>Counting Games for Preschool</h1>
  <p class="lead" style="font-size:20px;max-width:44em">Play <strong>{game.title}</strong>: tap each apple once to count it
    for a hungry hedgehog, then say how many. Six small rounds, about 3 minutes, free.</p>

  <GamePlayer game={game} topDifficulty={skill.topDifficulty}
    posterAlt={`${game.title} game screen: apples on a picnic blanket, a hedgehog, a number badge, a Done bell, Hazel the owl and a row of baby animals`} />

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>What is Hedgehog Picnic?</h2>
    <p>Hedgehog Picnic is a gentle counting game set on a picnic blanket in a sunny meadow. Three friends come along:
      a hedgehog who wants apples, a duck who collects pebbles and a mouse who loves acorns. Your child taps each
      object once. It lights up, a ring stays under it, and a big number shows the count so far. Then the friend asks
      "How many?" and your child picks the number. Every solved round brings a new baby animal to your child's parade.</p>

    <h2>What kids learn</h2>
    <p>The game practices counting a group of objects and knowing that the last number said tells how many there are.
      Children also practice touching each object once, keeping track of what is counted, and making a group of a given size. The rounds grow in four small steps:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>2 to 5 objects in one neat row</li>
      <li>5 to 10 objects in tidy rows of five</li>
      <li>4 to 10 objects in a loose pile, where your child rings the Done bell when every object is counted</li>
      <li>"Give me four apples!": your child moves that many apples into a basket, from 3 up to 10, then taps Done</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <p>Children in preschool and Pre-K, usually ages 3 to 5, who are starting to count things they can see. There is
      nothing to read: a short voice line says what to do, and the numbers are spoken as your child taps. It works best
      on a tablet, a computer or a phone held sideways.</p>

    <h2>How to play</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Press Play, then tap the big button to start.</li>
      <li>Tap each object once. A ring shows it has been counted, and tapping it again does not add to the count.</li>
      <li>When the friend asks "How many?", tap the number. You can also press a number key on a keyboard.</li>
      <li>Not sure? After a first wrong choice the objects light up one by one so your child can count along. After a
        second, a voice counts with them out loud. Your child still picks the number.</li>
      <li>Solve the round and a baby animal joins the parade.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Your parade</h2>
    <p>Every solved round adds a baby animal to your child's parade: a chick, a lamb, a calf, a piglet, a bunny or a
      little goat, always in that order. The parade is kept on this device only. There is no score, no timer, no red
      mark and no failure screen, and nothing is ever taken away.</p>

    <h2>Why it helps</h2>
    <p>Young children often know the counting words before they can use them to count things. Touching each object once,
      hearing each number and then saying how many links the words to real amounts. The game starts with small, neat rows,
      steps up after two rounds solved on the first try and steps back after two rounds in a row that needed the counting out loud, so each
      round stays within reach. It is one small part of learning to count, best next to counting real things at home.</p>

    <h2>Tips for parents</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Play the first round together. Point at each apple as your child taps and say the numbers out loud.</li>
      <li>Skipping an object or counting one twice is normal at this age. Count again slowly, side by side.</li>
      <li>Count real things too: spoons at dinner, stairs, socks in the wash.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">More to play: see all <a href={url('math-games/')}>free math games</a>, find matching pairs in
      <a href={url('logic-games/memory-match-for-preschool/')}>Memory Games for Preschool and Kindergarten</a>, or, for older
      children, try <a href={url('math-games/addition-for-grade-1/')}>Addition for Grade 1</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <AdSlot id="game-bottom" />
</Base>
